    Linked (not copied) source files: the same files Maple.WinUI.csproj
    compiles, compiled a second time into this assembly. Keeps a single
    source of truth while avoiding the WinUI dependency — see the header
    comment above. `SidecarStore.cs` is included too (real-file I/O, no
    WinUI/WinRT dependency) so its atomic-write/read path gets real-file
    coverage per CLAUDE.md's "no mocks for the sidecar layer" rule.
    `SelectionLogic.cs` (#2634) is a standalone, generic-over-item-type
    static class with zero WinUI dependency — unlike PhotoItem, which lives
    in a file (EditSessionViewModel.Library.cs) that also carries the
    WinUI-dependent bulk of EditSessionViewModel, so it isn't linkable here.

    `Services\FileOperations\*.cs` (#2632, the relocate/trash/folder-CRUD
    service) is WinUI-free by construction — plain System.IO plus one
    shell32 P/Invoke (RecycleBinService.cs) that compiles on any TFM
    regardless of OS, so it links the same way. `DiagLog.cs` is pulled in
    too since LocalFileOperations' best-effort delete paths log through it
    and it has no WinUI dependency of its own. `SingleFlightGate.cs` (#2743
    review fix) is a plain bool-backed guard with zero dependencies of its
    own, linked individually the same way `DiagLog.cs` is.

    `AppSettings.cs` (#2948) is plain System.IO/System.Text.Json plus the
    DPAPI helpers noted on the PackageReference above — no WinUI dependency
    of its own, linked the same way `DiagLog.cs`/`SingleFlightGate.cs` are.
    Its own tests use real file I/O against the real
    %LOCALAPPDATA%\Maple\settings.json (CLAUDE.md's "no mocks" reasoning
    applies here too: the invariant under test IS about what actually lands
    on disk), backing up and restoring whatever was there before/after each
    test.

    `Services\StorageReport.cs` (MN3, #3052) is the Settings window's
    byte-formatting + best-effort directory-size probe — plain System.IO,
    no WinUI dependency, linked the same way `DiagLog.cs`/`AppSettings.cs`
    are. Its directory tests use real temp directories (the filesystem IS
    the contract), mirroring the AppSettings reasoning above.

    `MapleUI\Atoms\MuiTimestampFormatter.cs` (Maple.UI Windows-phase atoms) is
    the Timestamp atom's date-math split into a plain C# static class with no
    WinUI/WinRT dependency of its own (System/System.Globalization only), for
    the same reason as the files above — it's the one part of that atom this
    project can actually exercise without a live Window.

    `MapleUI\Atoms\MuiRemoteImageLoader.cs`, `MuiAvatarPalette.cs`, and
    `MuiToastAutoDismissTimer.cs` (Maple.UI Windows-phase atoms wave 2,
    #3012) are the same split for the Remote Image/Avatar/Toast atoms: each
    is plain C# with zero WinUI/WinRT dependency
    (System/System.Threading(.Tasks) only), so the tiered-load state
    machine, the deterministic name-hash palette pick and initials
    extraction, and the injectable-tick auto-dismiss countdown are all
    exercisable here without a live Window — the one part of each of those
    atoms CI can actually verify. `MuiQrMatrix.cs` (MN4, #3053) is the QR
    Code atom's payload-to-module-matrix seam over the QRCoder package
    (referenced above) — it replaced the deleted placeholder pattern when
    the atom became a real, scannable code.

    `MapleUI\Molecules\MuiSliderMath.cs`, `MuiDragBarMath.cs`,
    `MuiColorWheelMath.cs`, `MuiPad2DMath.cs`, `MuiChipRowLogic.cs`, and
    `MuiFrameTimeClassifier.cs` (Maple.UI Windows-phase molecules L1 wave
    N3a, #3012) are the same split for the Slider/Living Slider, Drag Bar,
    Color Wheel, 2-D Pad, Chip Row, and Frame-time HUD molecules: each is
    plain C# (System/System.Collections.Generic only, namespace `Maple.UI`
    per the molecules' own convention rather than `Maple.UI.Atoms`), so the
    value<->position/percent math, tick layout, pointer<->hue/saturation and
    pointer<->x/y math, chip selection/draft-trim logic, and frame-budget
    classification are all exercisable here without a live Window.

    `MapleUI\Molecules\MuiPopoverMath.cs`, `MuiMenuNavMath.cs`,
    `MuiCommandMenuMath.cs`, `MuiToolbarMath.cs`, `MuiAvatarGroupMath.cs`,
    `MuiPlotMath.cs`, `MuiVectorscopeMath.cs`, `MuiCurvePlotMath.cs`,
    `MuiConnectionGraphMath.cs`, `MuiHeatmapMath.cs`, and
    `MediaTransportState.cs` (Maple.UI Windows-phase molecules L1 wave N3b,
    #3012 — overlays/menus, structure, data plots, media) are the same
    split for all 23 of that wave's molecules: each is plain C#
    (System/System.Collections.Generic/System.Linq only, namespace
    `Maple.UI`), so the popover placement math, menu keyboard-nav wrap
    math, command-palette filter/clamp, toolbar overflow split, avatar-
